; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Fibonacci That Remembers
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     recursion, memoization, property lists, speed
; SUMMARY:  Makes the recursive Fibonacci procedure fast by writing each answer into a property list the first time it is found.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   The plain recursive FIB works out the same small answers again and
;   again (see fibonacci-tree-of-calls.lgo). MEMOFIB keeps a notebook:
;   a property list called "fibnotes. Before doing any work it looks in
;   the notebook. If the answer is there it outputs it at once. If not,
;   it works it out, writes it down, and then outputs it.
;   The program races the two versions and prints how many calls and
;   how many milliseconds each one needed.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run and compare the two races.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the line
;        pprop "fibnotes :n :answer
;      and run. Open the Watch panel and add  plist "fibnotes
;      Each time you press F5, one more answer is written down. Notice
;      the order: fib 2 is saved first, then 3, then 4. The deepest
;      call finishes first.
;   3. Look at the Call Stack at that moment: it is only as tall as :n,
;      not the huge bushy tree of the slow version.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * memoization: remembering answers to avoid repeated work
;   * property lists with PPROP, GPROP and PLIST
;   * measuring speed with TIMEMILLI
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Raise the race to fib 24 for the slow version. Be patient.
;   * Ask for memofib 90. The slow version would never finish, and the
;     answer is so big that Logo switches to e+ notation to print it.
;   * Print plist "fibnotes after a run to see the whole notebook.
;   * Put a ; in front of the ERPL line and run twice. The second
;     memofib race needs only one call. Why?
; ============================================================

make "calls 0

; The slow, forgetful version, for comparison.
to slowfib :n
  make "calls :calls + 1
  if :n < 2 [output :n]
  output (slowfib :n - 1) + (slowfib :n - 2)
end

; The remembering version. GPROP outputs the empty list when nothing
; has been stored, so EMPTYP tells us whether we have seen :n before.
to memofib :n
  make "calls :calls + 1
  if :n < 2 [output :n]
  localmake "saved gprop "fibnotes :n
  if not emptyp :saved [output :saved]
  localmake "answer (memofib :n - 1) + (memofib :n - 2)
  pprop "fibnotes :n :answer
  output :answer
end

; Runs one version, timing it and counting its calls. The procedure
; to run is passed in by name, so the same race works for both.
to race :name :n
  make "calls 0
  localmake "start timemilli
  localmake "result invoke :name :n
  localmake "took timemilli - :start
  print (sentence :name :n "= :result)
  print (sentence "|   | :calls [calls,] :took [milliseconds])
end

; Start each run with an empty notebook, otherwise the race is unfair.
erpl "fibnotes
print [The race, fib 22 both ways:]
race "slowfib 22
race "memofib 22
print []
print [The remembering version can go much further:]
race "memofib 70
print []
print [Some of what is written in the notebook:]
foreach [10 20 30 40] [print (sentence "fib ? "= gprop "fibnotes ?)]
